The admiring responses posted thus far in the thread are certainly fair
and appropriate if we consider the various already mentioned facts.

But we're talking about the quality of the actual code base, not about
exculpating explanations how it got as it is.

It was badly written, even if compared to the standards at that time.
Most of the Good Style paradigms and the "Don't!"'s where already
present and widely known in the 1970's.

It is still badly written, the code base is the same, the language is
the same. The difference is that newer code changes are written in a
more disciplined way as the old parts. But the rotten base is the same.

Of course, the more and the closer you look at a dung heap the more
interesting it is; you see the details, admire the structure, detect
and understand the fauna, etc. It's still a dung heap.

That said; I am glad that the Devteam seems to have put a huge emphasis
on stability during the evolution of the game, abstaining from extreme
featuritis. Given Nethack's stability and functional deepness we don't
really need to peek into the horrible code base or overly praise it.

